#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116072} :activity/id #uuid "44ee8e90-06fb-5914-8a24-13b8cd43fdef" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050819 :topic/id #uuid "68fac9fb-a6fb-5c4b-a510-881f3f9dcbd6" :topic/math-academy-id 1685} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116072 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116550 :tutorial/content "The properties of indefinite integrals also apply to definite integrals. For example, to evaluate the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{2}^{3} 4 x \\, \\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwe can use the constant multiple rule and factor the constant out of the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{3}4x\\,\\text{d}x & =4{\\int }_{2}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =2({3}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =2(9-4) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, the constant multiple rule holds for any constant $k$ and any definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{a}^{b}kf(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =k{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bdf78ae3-dc9e-5db4-a6a4-ac8883d6ab7a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 771 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "42942332-9de2-50a2-84a2-31cc9820d6cd" :step/math-academy-id 1479 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116073}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116073 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115209 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115032 :question/id #uuid "4036785f-d99b-5c01-81e9-edfb93cc1f0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-413"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eaea1ecf-d6b9-5807-8776-17359e94336b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050057}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314865} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314866}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Using the Constant Multiple Rule"} :step/id #uuid "21ed1c8e-2514-5eae-bb70-556cf141e5f0" :step/math-academy-id 1480 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116074}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116074 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116551 :tutorial/content "We can also use the sum rule to compute the definite integral of the sum of two functions. For example, to evaluate the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{1}^{2} (x+x^2) \\, \\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwe can split up the integral into a sum of two integrals and evaluate each integral separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(x+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-1}{2}+\\frac{8-1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{a}^{b}(f(x)+g(x))\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{a}^{b}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4a0dd6d3-abec-56e8-a3b1-a812dfb45c5a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 773 :tutorial/title "Integrating the Sum of Two Functions"} :step/id #uuid "a617fa95-33e7-5e87-86d9-7d7d9736b369" :step/math-academy-id 1482 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116075}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116075 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115210 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115039 :question/id #uuid "6ecec0d3-1714-550b-bce2-88ead5abf2f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-414"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f236feb9-761b-544b-b34a-19eb46c28988" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050321}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297439} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341948}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Integral of the Sum of Two Functions Using the Sum Rule"} :step/id #uuid "c61f0b67-9fc5-5ae1-b73c-67633bb488dc" :step/math-academy-id 1483 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116076}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116076 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116552 :tutorial/content "To compute the integral of the sum of two functions multiplied by constants, we can combine the addition rule and the constant multiple rule. For example, to compute the integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^1 (3x^2 + 6x)\\,\\textrm d x ,\n$$\n\n we can split up the sum and factor out the constants, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{x}^{2}+6x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}6x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+6{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute the two resulting integrals using the usual methods.\n\nIn general, for functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and constants $c$ and $k$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{a}^{b}(cf(x)+kg(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =c{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+k{\\int }_{a}^{b}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "244b2afb-58eb-526d-ba0c-7ff36d5b834f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 775 :tutorial/title "Combining the Constant Multiple and Sum Rules"} :step/id #uuid "883460b7-eab8-5c86-9349-144425d70c90" :step/math-academy-id 1485 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116077}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116077 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115211 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115041 :question/id #uuid "d5d4992c-4c68-5d30-b7b7-a5a89e4cc3e4" :question/math-academy-id "e-415"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5cff6bd5-aeb6-55ed-a44b-fca322e73dd8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314941}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Integral of a Polynomial Function"} :step/id #uuid "33986316-e319-566e-9931-5531866da50b" :step/math-academy-id 1486 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116078}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116078 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115212 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115047 :question/id #uuid "0347b53a-4f7c-587e-9232-aa9be2065576" :question/math-academy-id "e-416"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66c5a147-0100-5f4b-a221-25b8674b2115" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314944}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Integral of a Sum of Two Functions"} :step/id #uuid "8300db68-e37d-5905-a6e3-75198133f507" :step/math-academy-id 1488}] :activity/title "The Sum and Constant Multiple Rules for Definite Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115456}]}
